{{ElectionsNE}}
The '''1996 United States Senate election in Nebraska''' was held on November 5, 1996. Incumbent Democratic U.S. Senator [[J. James Exon]] decided to retire instead of seeking a fourth term. Republican nominee [[Chuck Hagel]] won the open seat by 14 points, defeating incumbent Democratic governor [[Ben Nelson]], who later held Nebraska’s other Senate seat from 2001 to 2013, serving alongside Hagel until he retired from the Senate in 2009.

As of {{CURRENTYEAR}}, this is the last time an incumbent governor failed to hold a Senate seat for his party. This was also the first time since [[Carl Curtis]] was elected to his final term [[1972 United States Senate election in Nebraska|in 1972]] that a Republican was elected to either of Nebraska's Senate seats.{{efn|[[David Karnes]] was appointed to the Senate seat left vacant following the 1987 death of Democratic Senator [[Ed Zorinsky]] but was defeated in his bid for [[1988 United States Senate election in Nebraska|a full term]] by Democratic candidate and former Governor [[Bob Kerrey]].}}

