===Nomination===
No candidates filed for the Libertarian Party's nomination, but a primary election was still held, and 60 write-in votes were cast. Of those, 14 votes were cast for Republican [[Chuck Hagel]] and 11 for former State Senator [[John DeCamp]]. The Nebraska Board of State Canvassers voted 3-2 to certify Hagel as the nominee,<ref>{{Cite news|last1=Kotok|first1=C. David|last2=Brennan|first2=Joe|title=Hagel Mulls Libertarian Decision|newspaper=[[Omaha World-Herald]]|location=[[Omaha, Nebraska]]|date=June 26, 1996|page=24|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-grand-island-independent/166662358/|access-date=February 25, 2025}}</ref> but Hagel ultimately declined the nomination, which then went to DeCamp, who accepted it.<ref>{{Cite news|title=Senate campaign begins to heat up|newspaper=[[Star-Herald]]|location=[[Scottsbluff, Nebraska]]|date=July 31, 1996|page=3|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/star-herald/166664567/|access-date=February 25, 2025}}</ref> Though the [[Nebraska Republican Party]] filed a lawsuit seeking to block DeCamp's nomination, the lawsuit was dismissed by a state trial court judge and DeCamp was placed on the ballot.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Barrette|first=John|title=State GOP loses bid to remove candidates|newspaper=[[Lincoln Journal Star]]|location=[[Lincoln, Nebraska]]|date=September 13, 1996|page=3B|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/lincoln-journal-star/166664694/|access-date=February 25, 2025}}</ref>

